## 4. Dex

Add the relay's static client to Dex's configuration, with its one redirect URI:

```yaml
staticClients:
  - id: {{.ClientID}}
    name: patchy previews
    secretEnv: {{.SecretEnv}}
    redirectURIs:
      - https://{{.SignIn}}/dex/callback
```

Give Dex the secret in that environment variable from the Secret in its namespace (with Dex's Helm chart:
`envVars: [{name: {{.SecretEnv}}, valueFrom: {secretKeyRef: {name: {{.DexSecret}}, key: {{.DexSecretKey}}}}}]`),
then roll Dex:

```sh
kubectl apply -f {{.DexSecretFile}}
```

## 5. TLS

{{if .GatewayCert -}}
The gateway terminates TLS itself with one certificate for `*.{{.Suffix}}` and `{{.SignIn}}`, issued by cert-manager
and picked up again at every renewal without a restart.
{{- if .CertManager}}

Install cert-manager at the version patchy pins:

```sh
helm upgrade --install {{.CertManager.Release}} {{.CertManager.Chart}} --repo {{.CertManager.Repo}} \
  --version {{.CertManager.Version}} -n {{.CertManager.Namespace}} --create-namespace -f {{.CertManager.ValuesFile}}
```
{{- end}}
{{- if .Issuer}}

The Issuer answers the challenge with the `{{.DNS}}` DNS-01 solver. {{.DNSLeastPrivilege}}
{{- if .DNSCredentialWritten}}

Its credential is in `{{.DNSSecretFile}}`:

```sh
kubectl apply -f {{.DNSSecretFile}}
```
{{- else if .DNSCredentialNeeded}}

Its credential is Secret `{{.DNSSecret}}` (key `{{.DNSSecretKey}}`), which you create, from a file so it stays out of
your shell's history (or run the command again with `--dns-credentials-file` and apply the file it writes):

```sh
kubectl -n {{.Namespace}} create secret generic {{.DNSSecret}} --from-file={{.DNSSecretKey}}=<the credential's file>
```
{{- else}}

It takes no stored credential: cert-manager's own identity answers, so give that identity the grant above.
{{.AmbientIdentity}}

cert-manager lends its own identity only to a ClusterIssuer, so `{{.IssuerFile}}` is the ClusterIssuer
`{{.IssuerName}}`, not a namespaced Issuer: do not set `--issuer-ambient-credentials` instead, which would let every
namespace's Issuer spend that identity. It is cluster-wide either way: a Certificate in any namespace can name
`{{.IssuerName}}` and be issued any name the identity can answer for, so keep the grant to the two challenge records.
To keep it to this namespace instead, run the command again with a stored credential (`{{.AmbientStored}}` and
`--dns-credentials-file`): the recipe then writes a namespaced Issuer that reads it from a Secret.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

```sh
{{- if .Issuer}}
kubectl apply -f {{.IssuerFile}}
{{- end}}
kubectl apply -f {{.CertificateFile}}
kubectl -n {{.Namespace}} wait certificate/{{.CertificateName}} --for=condition=Ready --timeout=10m
```

`previewAuth.gateway.tls.secrets` names its Secret, `{{.CertificateSecret}}`. The two names sit on different
registrable domains, so usually in two DNS zones: the solver must be able to answer for both.
{{- else if .Cloudflare -}}
Cloudflare's edge holds both certificates; nothing in the cluster does, and cloudflared reaches the gateway inside the
cluster over plain HTTP.
{{- if .AdvancedCertificates}} You said the zones have Advanced Certificate Manager (`--cloudflare-acm`): order an
advanced certificate covering `*.{{.Suffix}}` in `{{.SuffixSite}}` and one covering `{{.SignIn}}` in `{{.SignInSite}}`
(or turn on Total TLS) before the records in step 7 are proxied, or browsers are shown an invalid certificate.
{{- else}} Universal SSL, free on every plan, covers each zone's apex and first-level names: `*.{{.Suffix}}` and
`{{.SignIn}}` are both, so there is nothing to order.
{{- end}} Turn on **Always Use HTTPS** in both zones.
{{- else if .Certificate -}}
```sh
kubectl apply -f {{.CertificateFile}}
kubectl -n {{.Namespace}} wait certificate/{{.CertificateName}} --for=condition=Ready --timeout=10m
```

Both Ingresses name its Secret, `{{.CertificateSecret}}`. Put no `cert-manager.io/*issuer` annotation on either: a
second Certificate for the same Secret would fight this one.
{{- else if .Route -}}
Your Gateway `{{.GatewayNamespace}}/{{.GatewayName}}` terminates TLS. Give it one HTTPS listener for `*.{{.Suffix}}`
{{- if .GatewaySection}} (the listener `{{.GatewaySection}}` the previews' route names){{end}} and one for
`{{.SignIn}}`, each with a certificate (the wildcard never covers the sign-in host), and allow routes from namespace
`{{.Namespace}}`. Then route the sign-in host to the gateway:

```sh
kubectl apply -f {{.SignInRouteFile}}
```
{{- else -}}
The edge terminates TLS: it must hold a certificate for `*.{{.Suffix}}` and one for `{{.SignIn}}` (the wildcard never
covers the sign-in host).
{{- if .EdgeClass}} Both Ingresses take the chart's edge class: set `edgeIngressClass.create: true` in your
values if it is not already, and add the two ACM certificates' ARNs to `edgeIngressClass.certificateARNs` (the ALB
picks one by SNI).{{end}}
{{- end}}

## 6. Install
{{- if .Tailscale}}

The gateway's device joins the tailnet through the Tailscale operator. In the tailnet policy, let the operator own the
previews' tag and let viewers reach that device on 443 alone (put whoever views previews in `group:preview-viewers`;
each still signs in):

```json
{{.TailscalePolicy}}```

Grants and ACLs only add access, so this grant restricts nothing while another rule reaches the device. A new tailnet
ships with an allow-all rule (the grant `{"src": ["*"], "dst": ["*"], "ip": ["*"]}`, or the ACL
`{"action": "accept", "src": ["*"], "dst": ["*:*"]}`): while it stands, every member, shared-in user and tagged device
reaches `{{.TailscaleTag}}` on every port, 80 (the gateway's plain-HTTP listener) included. Remove it, or narrow it so
that no rule but the one above has `{{.TailscaleTag}}` (or `*`) as a destination.
{{- if .TailscaleOperator}}

Create an OAuth client in the Tailscale admin console with the write scopes the operator's guide
(https://tailscale.com/kb/1236/kubernetes-operator) names, tagged `{{.TailscaleOperatorTag}}`. Put its ID and secret
in two files, never on a command line, then:

```sh
kubectl create namespace {{.TailscaleNamespace}}
kubectl -n {{.TailscaleNamespace}} create secret generic {{.OAuthSecret}} \
  --from-file=client_id=<the client ID's file> --from-file=client_secret=<the client secret's file>
helm upgrade --install {{.TailscaleOperator.Release}} {{.TailscaleOperator.Chart}} --repo {{.TailscaleOperator.Repo}} \
  --version {{.TailscaleOperator.Version}} -n {{.TailscaleOperator.Namespace}} -f {{.TailscaleOperator.ValuesFile}}
```
{{- end}}

Then patchy:
{{- else if .Cloudflare}}

cloudflared runs in namespace `{{.CloudflaredNamespace}}`, which its manifest creates, with the tunnel credentials
mounted from Secret `{{.CloudflaredSecret}}` and egress to DNS, the gateway and Cloudflare (443, and 7844 over TCP and
QUIC) alone:

```sh
kubectl apply -f {{.CloudflaredFile}} -f {{.CloudflaredSecretFile}}
kubectl -n {{.CloudflaredNamespace}} rollout status deployment/cloudflared
```

It sends `*.{{.Suffix}}` and `{{.SignIn}}` to `{{.GatewayService}}` and answers 404 for any other name. Then patchy:
{{- end}}

```sh
kubectl apply -f {{.RelaySecretFile}}
helm upgrade {{.Release}} {{.ChartRef}} -n {{.Namespace}}{{if .ChartVersion}} --version {{.ChartVersion}}{{end}} \
  -f <your values> -f {{.ValuesFile}}
```

One upgrade: the gateway needs no permit stage. `previewController.config.apiServerCIDR: {{.APIServerCIDR}}`
{{- if eq .APIServerCIDR "auto"}} is looked up at render time, so it needs `helm upgrade` against the cluster (not
`helm template` or a GitOps render, which must set the kubernetes Service's `/32` instead){{else}} is the API server
address the preview-controller may reach{{end}}.

## 7. DNS
{{- if .Cloudflare}}

Two proxied records, each a CNAME to `{{.TunnelID}}.cfargotunnel.com`:

- `*.{{.Suffix}}`
- `{{.SignIn}}`

```sh
cloudflared tunnel route dns {{.TunnelID}} '*.{{.Suffix}}'
```

`cloudflared tunnel route dns` writes into the zone of the `cert.pem` that `cloudflared tunnel login` chose, so it
creates the wildcard when you chose `{{.SuffixSite}}`. Create the sign-in host's record in `{{.SignInSite}}`'s DNS
settings by hand (a proxied CNAME `{{.SignIn}}` to `{{.TunnelID}}.cfargotunnel.com`), or run `cloudflared tunnel login`
again for that zone and `cloudflared tunnel --origincert <its cert.pem> route dns {{.TunnelID}} {{.SignIn}}`.

Then, in `{{.SuffixSite}}`, add a Cache Rule that bypasses the cache for every request whose host ends in
`.{{.Suffix}}`: Cloudflare honours the gateway's `private`, but a rule that caches by file extension does not, and
would serve one viewer's preview to another. Keep the allowlist at Cloudflare too, if you want one (a WAF custom rule,
or Cloudflare Access): `previewAuth.gateway.forwardedHops` is 1 (Cloudflare's edge appends the viewer's address to
`X-Forwarded-For`; cloudflared adds none), and `inboundCIDRs` stays empty. A proxied record resolves to Cloudflare's
addresses, not the cluster's, so `patchy check previews` skips its DNS line: check both records in the dashboard.
{{- else}}

Point two records at the edge's address:

- `*.{{.Suffix}}`
- `{{.SignIn}}`

{{if .Tailscale -}}
The address is the tailnet device `{{.TailscaleHostname}}`'s, once the operator has created it (`tailscale ip -4
{{.TailscaleHostname}}` on any machine in the tailnet, or the admin console). MagicDNS takes no wildcard or custom
records, so these are records in your public DNS that point at a tailnet address: only the tailnet can reach it.
{{- if .TailscaleAddresses}} You gave {{range $i, $a := .TailscaleAddresses}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$a}}`{{end}}
(`previewAuth.gateway.publishedAddresses`, which `patchy check previews` compares DNS with).
{{- else}} Then run the command again with `--tailscale-address <address>`, so
`previewAuth.gateway.publishedAddresses` records it for `patchy check previews`.
{{- end}}

The gateway sees the tailnet proxy's address, not the viewer's, so `previewAuth.gateway.forwardedHops` is 0 and
`inboundCIDRs` stays empty: the tailnet policy in step 6 is the allowlist, once no allow-all rule is left in it.
{{- else if .GatewayTLS -}}
The address is the gateway's own load balancer (`kubectl -n {{.Namespace}} get service {{.GatewayService}} -o
jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0]}'` once it is provisioned).
{{- if .LBClass}} It comes from the load balancer class `{{.LBClass}}`.{{end}}
`externalTrafficPolicy: Local` keeps the viewer's address as the gateway's peer, so
`previewAuth.gateway.forwardedHops` is 0
{{- if .InboundCIDRs}} and the load balancer admits only {{range $i, $c := .InboundCIDRs}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$c}}`{{end}}
(`loadBalancerSourceRanges`, checked again by the gateway){{end}}.
{{- if .ServiceLB}}

On k3s, ServiceLB gives the Service the nodes' addresses and needs port 443 free on them: with k3s's bundled Traefik
also on 443, the Service stays pending. Use `--edge existing --ingress-class traefik` there, or give it an address of
its own (MetalLB). On k3d, the cluster's load balancer must also publish 443 (`-p "<address>:443:443@loadbalancer"`
when the cluster is created).
{{- end}}
{{- else if .Route -}}
The address is your Gateway's (`kubectl -n {{.GatewayNamespace}} get gateway {{.GatewayName}}`). An HTTPRoute
publishes no address of its own, so also set `previewAuth.gateway.publishedAddresses` to it in your values for
`patchy check previews`.
{{- else -}}
The address is the Ingress controller's load balancer (`kubectl -n {{.Namespace}} get ingress {{.GatewayService}}`
once it is admitted). `previewAuth.gateway.forwardedHops: 1` holds when the controller sees the client's address
(its Service has `externalTrafficPolicy: Local`, or it trusts the PROXY protocol); behind a cloud L7 load balancer
that also appends `X-Forwarded-For`, it is 2.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

## 8. Check

```sh
patchy check previews -n {{.Namespace}}
```

Every line should PASS before the first intent's preview is opened.

## Rolling back

In reverse order, all by deletion:

1. Set `previewAuth.gateway.enabled: false` (or `preview.enabled: false`) and run the same `helm upgrade`.
{{- if .GatewayCert}}
2. `kubectl delete -f {{.CertificateFile}}`{{if .Issuer}} and `kubectl delete -f {{.IssuerFile}}`{{end}}
{{- if .Tailscale}}, then remove the device `{{.TailscaleHostname}}` from the tailnet if
   the operator has not{{end}}.
{{- else if .Cloudflare}}
2. `kubectl delete -f {{.CloudflaredFile}}`, which deletes namespace `{{.CloudflaredNamespace}}` and the tunnel
   credentials' Secret with it.
{{- else if .Certificate}}
2. `kubectl delete -f {{.CertificateFile}}`
{{- else if .Route}}
2. `kubectl delete -f {{.SignInRouteFile}}`
{{- else}}
2. Remove the certificates from the edge, if nothing else uses them.
{{- end}}
3. Remove the two DNS records.{{if .Cloudflare}} Then delete the tunnel on your workstation (`cloudflared tunnel delete
   {{.TunnelID}}`): its credentials stop working everywhere they were copied.{{end}}
4. Remove the static client from Dex, and delete the Secrets (`kubectl delete {{if .Cloudflare}}--ignore-not-found {{end}}-f secrets/`).
{{- if or .CertManager .TailscaleOperator}}
   Uninstall what this recipe installed if nothing else uses it:{{if .TailscaleOperator}} `helm uninstall
   {{.TailscaleOperator.Release}} -n {{.TailscaleOperator.Namespace}}`{{end}}{{if and .CertManager .TailscaleOperator}},{{end}}
   {{- if .CertManager}} `helm uninstall {{.CertManager.Release}} -n {{.CertManager.Namespace}}` (its CRDs stay, and
   with them every Certificate in the cluster){{end}}.
{{- end}}
5. Delete what the chart keeps on uninstall, as `docs/intents/preview-sign-in.md` lists it.
